## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical thinking on append-heavy data structures, cache-aware trade-offs, and I/O modeling under realistic constraints.

## Key Concepts
- Two-tier indexing
- Amortized cost analysis
- Cache & I/O modeling
- Rebuild strategies and hot/cold separation

## Code Example
```javascript
class TieredIndex {
  constructor(B) { this.B=B; this.t1=new Map(); this.t2=new Map(); }
  append(blockId, offset) { this.t1.set(blockId, offset); /* trigger rebuild when needed */ }
  lookup(blockId) { if (this.t1.has(blockId)) return this.t1.get(blockId); return this.t2.get(blockId); }
  rebuild() { for (let [k,v] of this.t1) this.t2.set(k,v); this.t1.clear(); }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would this adapt if blocks could be deleted or updated? 
- How would you empirically validate the cost model with real workloads?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to extend classic shortest-paths to dynamic updates with a concrete, real-world constraint (only decreases) and to analyze practical performance in terms of amortized work and memory.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic single-source shortest paths with only edge decreases
- Amortized analysis and potential-based arguments
- Priority-queue relaxation propagation
- Memory-time trade-offs and localized updates
- Practical optimizations: skip non-on-SPT edges, batch updates, integer weights with Dial's algorithm

## Code Example

```javascript
class DynamicSSSP {
  constructor(graph, source) {
    this.graph = graph; // adjacency: {u: [{v, w}, ...]}
    this.n = Object.keys(graph).length;
    this.dist = Array(this.n).fill(Infinity);
    this.dist[source] = 0;
    this.pq = new MinHeap(); // custom min-heap with push/pop
    this.pq.push([0, source]);
    while (!this.pq.isEmpty()) {
      const [d, u] = this.pq.pop();
      if (d !== this.dist[u]) continue;
      for (const {v, w} of this.graph[u]) {
        if (this.dist[v] > d + w) {
          this.dist[v] = d + w;
          this.pq.push([this.dist[v], v]);
        }
      }
    }
  }
  decreaseEdge(u, v, newW) {
    // update edge weight in graph[u] item
    // assume newW <= oldW
    // attempt relaxation
    if (this.dist[u] + newW < this.dist[v]) {
      this.dist[v] = this.dist[u] + newW;
      this.pq.push([this.dist[v], v]);
      while (!this.pq.isEmpty()) {
        const [d, x] = this.pq.pop();
        if (d !== this.dist[x]) continue;
        for (const {to, w} of this.graph[x] || []) {
          if (this.dist[to] > d + w) {
            this.dist[to] = d + w;
            this.pq.push([this.dist[to], to]);
          }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt if many edges frequently decrease in a short window?
- How would you extend to multiple sources or all-pairs distances, and what would the complexity trade-offs look like?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of approximate quantiles with streaming data, a common constraint in production systems. It compares simple histograms to exact tracking and asks for concrete complexity and error bounds.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming histograms for quantiles
- Per-element O(1) updates, O(W) queries
- Error bounds from bucket width w

## Code Example
```javascript
function HistQuantile(m, W) {
  const bucket = new Array(W).fill(0);
  this.n = 0; this.m = m; this.W = W;
  const w = Math.ceil(m / W);
  this.update = (x) => { const idx = Math.min(Math.floor(x / w), W-1); bucket[idx]++; this.n++; };
  this.median = () => {
    let target = Math.floor(this.n / 2) + 1;
    let cum = 0; let i = 0; const bw = Math.ceil(m / W);
    for (; i < W; i++) { if (cum + bucket[i] >= target) break; cum += bucket[i]; }
    const L = i * bw; const left = target - cum;
    const est = left <= bucket[i] ? L + (left / bucket[i]) * bw : L + bw;
    return est;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adjust for skewed data?  
- How does choosing W affect error vs memory?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests the ability to design batched updates for a non-trivial dynamic property with practical performance guarantees.

## Key Concepts
- sqrt decomposition; block-level DP profiles; mergeable LIS state; amortized update costs.

## Code Example
```javascript
// sketch: recompute LIS for a block
function recomputeBlock(block) {
  const arr = block.values;
  // standard patience algorithm within block
  let tails = [];
  for (const x of arr) {
    let i = tails.findIndex(t => t >= x);
    if (i === -1) tails.push(x); else tails[i] = x;
  }
  block.lisLen = tails.length;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle large value ranges? Use coordinate compression; how to adapt if updates can be deletions? What are worst-case vs average-case guarantees?

## Why This Is Asked
This question assesses the ability to combine streaming sketches with efficient top-K data structures while reasoning about accuracy, space, and time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- **Streaming Algorithms:** Processing unbounded data with limited memory
- **Count-Min Sketch (CMS):** Probabilistic data structure for frequency estimation with false positives but no false negatives
- **Top-K Maintenance:** Using a min-heap to track the K most frequent elements efficiently
- **Amortized Analysis:** Bounding average cost over sequences of operations
- **Space-Time Trade-offs:** Comparing approximate vs. exact approaches

## Code Example
```javascript
class CountMinSketch {
  constructor(w, d) {
    this.w = w;
    this.d = d;
    this.table = Array.from({ length: d }, () => Array(w).fill(0));
  }

  hash(key, i) {
    const str = typeof key === 'string' ? key : String(key);
    let hash = 0;
    for (let j = 0; j < str.length; j++) {
      hash = (hash * 31 + str.charCodeAt(j)) >>> 0;
    }
    return (hash + i * 31) % this.w;
  }

  update(key) {
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      this.table[i][j]++;
    }
  }

  estimate(key) {
    let min = Infinity;
    for (let i = 0; i < this.d; i++) {
      const j = this.hash(key, i);
      min = Math.min(min, this.table[i][j]);
    }
    return min;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked

This question tests the ability to design streaming algorithms with bounded memory while computing exact results. It captures a common real-world problem: maintaining aggregate statistics over a sliding or growing dataset without storing all historical values.

## Key Concepts

- **Streaming data structures:** Using heaps to maintain top-K elements efficiently
- **Running sum:** Incremental updates avoid recomputing the sum after each batch
- **Time complexity:** O(log K) per element; batch processing takes O(B log K)
- **Memory usage:** O(K) vs O(N) for the exact method that stores all past values

## Code Example

```javascript
function topKSumStream(valueStream, K) {
  const heap = new MinHeap();
  let sum = 0;

  for (const batch of valueStream) {
    for (const v of batch) {
      if (heap.size() < K) {
        heap.push(v);
        sum += v;
      } else if (heap.size() > 0 && v > heap.peek()) {
        sum += v - heap.pop();
        heap.push(v);
      }
    }
    console.log("Top-K sum:", sum);
  }
}
```

## Asymptotic Analysis

| Metric | Our Solution | Exact Method |
|--------|--------------|--------------|
| Per-element update | O(log K) | O(1) |
| Batch time (size B) | O(B log K) | O(B) |
| Memory | O(K) | O(N) |

